  nodeName: G Proteins Bind to GLP-1R
  nodePreview: {fileID: 0}
  nodeNumber: 0
  nodeNexus: 1
  nodeType: 0
  nodeConstituents: 070000000600000008000000
  nodePositions: []
  nodeAbstract: 
  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: 'How do G Protein and GLP-1R meet?



'
    basic: G proteins move along the inside of the cell, attached to the membrane.
      It binds to 'open' pockets of GLP-1R's helices that are also on the membrane
      interior.
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "G gamma and sometimes G alpha are covalently linked to a hydrophobic
      lipid molecule. \nThis lipid anchor allows the G protein complex to move along
      the plasma membrane, and increases its chances of meeting GLP-1R.\n31 amino
      acids of the G protein complex interact with the binding pocket of activated
      GLP-1R. \nThe interaction between GLP-1R and G alpha promotes release of GDP
      from G alpha. \nFree GTP in the cytoplasm binds to the nucleotide binding pocket
      of G alpha, this apparent exchange is promoted by higher concentration of GTP
      in the cytoplasm than GDP."
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
